    "result_text": "The full test suite is green. \n\n## Summary\n\nThe change rejects a supplied `registrationDate` later than the server date with HTTP 400.\n\n**What I found:** This is the Spring PetClinic REST app. Owner registration happens in `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` (`POST /api/owners`), where `effectiveRegistrationDate` resolved the supplied date but accepted any value, including future dates. The `AcceptanceTest.errorRejectsFutureDate` test (posting `registrationDate: \"2999-01-01\"` and expecting 400) was already present but failing.\n\n**The fix** (`OwnerRestControllerV1.java:264`): in `effectiveRegistrationDate`, added a guard that throws `InvalidFieldsException(List.of(\"registrationDate\"))` when the supplied date is after `LocalDate.now()`. This reuses the established idiom already used for `telephone`/`email`/`postcode` validation \u2014 `InvalidFieldsException` is mapped to a 400 `ProblemDetail` by the existing `ExceptionControllerAdvice`. The check runs on the raw supplied date (before the weekend\u2192business-day roll), so it compares exactly what the client sent against the server date, and a `null` (omitted) date still defaults to today as before.\n\n**Verification:** `./mvnw test` \u2192 `BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` (the log also shows the advice firing: `Invalid field values at POST /api/owners: [registrationDate]`).",
